857,"def_epa_per_play":-0.3348},{"week":16,"opponent":"HOU","home":false,"pf":37.0,"pa":31.0,"off_snaps":70,"epa_per_play":0.3503,"pass_rate":0.5429,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6102,"def_epa_per_play":0.2438},{"week":17,"opponent":"BAL","home":true,"pf":3.0,"pa":38.0,"off_snaps":40,"epa_per_play":-0.5843,"pass_rate":0.525,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6667,"def_epa_per_play":0.3491}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2025,"team":"JAX","distance":0.5032,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Liam Coen"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYG","distance":0.5405,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Brian Daboll"},{"season":2025,"team":"DAL","distance":0.6131,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Brian Schottenheimer"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.6241,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CAR","distance":0.6289,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"}],"defense":[{"season":2024,"team":"DAL","distance":0.3977,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"LV","distance":0.6246,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Patrick Graham"},{"season":2020,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6383,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6639,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2023,"team":"WAS","distance":0.6719,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bengals went 4-11-1 in 2020 and were outscored 424 to 311. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 26th in EPA per play and the defense 26th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd highest empty formation usage and the most defensive linemen on the field in the league.","offense":"The Bengals offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd highest empty formation usage and the 2nd most receivers on the field in the league. Joe Burrow ran the passing game, with Tyler Boyd leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bengals had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Even its strongest area, success rate allowed, only ranked 16th, and it was 32nd in explosive play rate allowed. Sam Hubbard led the way with 2 sacks. Schematically, they had the most defensive linemen on the field in the league.","special":"The Bengals were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 79% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into running backs, about 8% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 51% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Joe Burrow played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,688 yards. Targets were spread around, with Tyler Boyd and Tee Higgins leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Giovani Bernard and Joe Mixon. On defense, Carl Lawson led the team with 5.5 sacks and Jessie Bates III picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Zac Taylor called the offense as head coach and Lou Anarumo called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Jaguars under Liam Coen and the 2024 Giants under Brian Daboll. Their defense looked most like the 2024 Cowboys and the 2022 Raiders under Patrick Graham.","trends":"The offense went from 29th in EPA per play in 2019 to 26th in 2020, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 31st in EPA allowed per play in 2019 to 26th in 2020, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 11% to 0%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2020. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}